<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Murshid Quli Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1660–1727</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Murshid Quli Khan was a Mughal official appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwan">diwan</a> (revenue minister) of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>. He moved the revenue office to the town he renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murshidabad">Murshidabad</a> and in 1717 also became governor. His efficient revenue system made Bengal the richest Mughal province, and his rule laid the foundations of the hereditary Nawabs of Bengal, who acted largely independently of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/murshid-quli-khan.jpg" alt="Murshid Quli Khan" width="320" height="251" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: W. H. Allen and Co. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pope1880BengalPres2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
